# Parameters reproducing the theory curve of Fig. 4b,
# Snoeijer & Andreotti, Annu. Rev. Fluid Mech. 45 (2013).
#
# Receding plate withdrawn from a silicone-oil bath (Delon et al. 2008):
# lengths in units of the capillary length l_gamma; microscopic angle =
# receding equilibrium angle. The two microscopic parameters are
# CALIBRATED against the vector-digitized theory curve of the figure
# (data/fig4b-digitized/), not independently measured:
#   theta_mic_deg = 53.46  fixed by the static foot Delta(Ca->0) =
#                          sqrt(2(1 - sin(theta_e))) = 0.627;
#   slip = 7.46e-6         fixed by the digitized fold Ca* = 10.54e-3 (the
#                          prediction is logarithmically sensitive to slip,
#                          so this absorbs the O(1) microscopic-cutoff
#                          choices; it corresponds to ~11 nm for silicone
#                          oil, l_gamma ~ 1.5 mm).
#
# Because slip is calibrated directly to Ca*, reproducing Ca* back out of
# the traced branch is NOT a test -- it is circular by construction. The
# genuine out-of-sample checks are: the fold HEIGHT Delta* = 1.440 vs 1.446
# digitized (0.4%, not fit to), and the full branch shape -- including the
# upper-branch approach to the Landau-Levich asymptote -- which involves no
# further free parameters (see img/fig4b-reproduction.png and
# postProcess/compare-c-python.py).

geometry = vertical
theta_mic_deg = 53.46
slip = 7.46e-6
mu_r = 0.0
grav = 1.0

H_match = 5.0
smax_cap = 100.0
rtol = 1.0e-10
atol = 1.0e-12

Ca_start = 1.0e-6
Delta_max = 3.7
Ca_stop_min = 1.0e-4
max_points = 2000
verbose = 1
